### 1. Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence indicates that while face masks are an important tool for reducing and mitigating the transmission of SARS-CoV-2, they do not completely stop the spread of the virus. Scientific studies show that masks—even when completely sealed—cannot fully block the transmission of viral droplets or aerosols. Furthermore, health organizations like the WHO have clarified that masks alone do not provide complete protection, and researchers emphasize that masks serve to slow or mitigate transmission rather than halt it entirely. The real-world effectiveness of masks is also highly variable, depending on the filtration efficiency of the specific mask type (with cloth masks performing poorly compared to surgical masks or N95 respirators) and requiring near-universal community compliance to significantly impact the epidemiological curve. Because the evidence is clear and sufficient, no further investigation is required.

### 3. Conclusion
Based on the fact-check, the claim that wearing face masks will stop the spread of COVID-19 is `refuted`. There is a clear scientific distinction between mitigating the transmission of a virus and stopping it entirely. The evidence demonstrates that while masks are effective at reducing the release of respiratory droplets (source control) and slowing the spread of the virus, they do not completely block all viral particles and cannot single-handedly stop the transmission of COVID-19.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Scientific evidence and public health guidelines demonstrate that while face masks help mitigate and slow the transmission of SARS-CoV-2, they do not completely stop the spread of the virus. A study indexed on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33087517/) confirms that masks cannot completely block the transmission of viral droplets or aerosols, even when fully sealed. Furthermore, official guidelines from the World Health Organization, as reported by [Vox](https://www.vox.com/2020/6/6/21282108/masks-for-covid-19-world-health-organization-guidelines-cloth-n95), clarified that masks alone do not provide complete protection, and research compiled by the [Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine](https://www.cebm.net/covid-19/masking-lack-of-evidence-with-politics/) highlights that masks are evaluated for reducing transmission rates rather than halting them entirely. The real-world effectiveness of masks also varies significantly based on the type of mask used, as detailed in a study on [PMC](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7553287/), and requires near-universal community compliance to be highly effective, according to a study in [PLOS ONE](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0240785). Therefore, the claim is refuted because masks serve to reduce and slow transmission rather than completely stopping it.